;; FH-BEHAVIOR-006 — a command reaches its explicit semantic target and NOTHING
;; else, and every other outcome is a visible refusal.
;;
;; The decoy is the point of the delivery case. Two behaviors of the same
;; registered type are live at once, under DISTINCT caller-authored target ids;
;; the command names one. If addressing were derived from render position, from
;; the behavior id, or from a document query, the decoy would be reachable —
;; so the assertion is symmetric: the named node carries the mark, and the decoy
;; carries nothing at all.
;;
;; The refusals are the bounded half. A command is never queued for a target
;; that is absent, never delivered to whichever of two claimants mounted last,
;; and never forwarded to an operation the behavior did not register. Each is a
;; typed refusal that performs NO host work — the transcript is unchanged and
;; the DOM is unchanged after every one of them.
;;
;; `:after-teardown` closes the loop with FH-BEHAVIOR-005: once the connection is
;; released the same command that worked a moment ago is refused, because the
;; index holds nothing. A future mount is driven by state and config, or by a
;; fresh event — never by a retained imperative request.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-006"
 :fh/law
 "A `:re-frame.freehand.host/command` effect reaches the ONE live connection claiming its explicit semantic target and nothing else — a live decoy under a different id is untouched — and an absent target, an ambiguous target, an unregistered operation and a malformed command are each refused with a typed diagnostic, performing no host work and queueing nothing."

 :fx-id :re-frame.freehand.host/command

 :delivered
 {:view      :pair
  :command   {:target :probe/one :op :mark :args {:label "hit"}}
  :marked    {:selector ".node[data-id='one']" :attribute "data-mark" :value "hit"}
  :untouched {:selector ".node[data-id='two']" :attribute "data-mark"}
  :lifecycle [:connect :connect :mark]}

 :refusals
 [{:note    "no live connection claims the target — refused, never queued for a future mount"
   :view    :pair
   :command {:target :probe/nobody :op :mark}
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

  {:note    "the behavior registered no such operation — the roster is finite"
   :view    :pair
   :command {:target :probe/one :op :teleport}
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

  {:note    "two live connections claim one id — refused rather than delivered to the last mounted"
   :view    :twins
   :command {:target :probe/same :op :mark}
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

  {:note    "a command names its target"
   :view    :pair
   :command {:op :mark}
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

  {:note    "and its operation"
   :view    :pair
   :command {:target :probe/one}
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

  {:note    "a command is a map"
   :view    :pair
   :command "mark it"
   :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}]

 :after-teardown
 {:command {:target :probe/one :op :mark :args {:label "late"}}
  :outcome :rf.error/behavior-command-refused}

 ;; The outward half: the context a behavior keeps is fenced to the connection
 ;; that minted it, so a host callback that outlives its node is inert.
 :fenced-dispatch
 {:event      [:probe/announced "live"]
  :while-live true
  :after-teardown false}}
